---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The Viv authors
// /vipyr: the user guide's front page. What vipyr is for, by topic, how to
// install it, a first call, and how its results are checked. Function pages
// are under /vipyr/api; everything they say comes from src/data/vipyr.json.
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import GalleryCard from '../../components/GalleryCard.astro';
import data from '../../data/vipyr.json';
import { REPO_PUBLIC, GITHUB } from '../../lib/repo.js';
import gallery from '../../data/gallery.json';
import { vipyrTree, TOPICS } from '../../lib/site';

const fns = Object.values(data.functions) as any[];
const fieldOf = (id: string) => data.fields.find((f: any) => f.id === id) as any;
const topics = TOPICS.map((t) => ({
  ...t,
  fams: t.families.map(fieldOf).filter(Boolean).filter((f: any) => fns.some((x) => x.family === f.id && x.kind !== 'coming')),
  examples: (gallery as any).entries.filter((e: any) => e.topic === t.id),
}));
// the cards under a topic: its first examples, then a link to the rest
const SHOWN = 4;
const built = fns.filter((f) => f.kind !== 'coming').length;

const FIRST = `import numpy as np
import vipyr as vp

# the optimal gain for a cart-pole, and the system run under it
A, B = vp.control.cartpole_model(h=1e-5)
r = vp.control.lqr(A=A, B=B, Q=np.diag([1.0, 1.0, 20.0, 1.0]), R=np.array([[0.05]]))
r.K          # array([[ -4.4721,  -8.2239, -63.0331, -16.0163]])
r.check()    # CheckReport(passed=True, ...): the program's own tests on this run`;
const OWN = `k = vp.load("kernels.viv")     # your own Viv file, compiled to C once and cached
k.sumsq(np.arange(4.0))          # each annotated def is a Python function on NumPy arrays`;
const TOC: [string, string][] = [
  ...TOPICS.map((t) => [t.id, t.title] as [string, string]),
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['first', 'A first call'],
  ['checked', 'How results are checked'],
  ['viv', 'The Viv language'],
];
---

<DocPage
  title="vipyr user guide"
  icon="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-icon.svg"
  image="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-icon-512.png"
  description="vipyr is the Viv language as a Python package: your own .viv files load as Python modules, and Viv's checked programs (Hartree-Fock, the shell model, option pricing, control, features on event tables) are Python functions."
  tree={vipyrTree(Astro.url.pathname)}
  toc={TOC}>
  <h1 class="ug-logo">
    <img class="brand-on-light" src="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-logo-light.svg" alt="vipyr" width="250" height="73" />
    <img class="brand-on-dark" src="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-logo-dark.svg" alt="vipyr" width="250" height="73" />
  </h1>
  <p class="lede">
    vipyr runs numerical code written in Viv from Python. Your own functions compile to C and come back as Python
    functions on NumPy arrays, and a library of programs checked against their reference codes is one import away.
  </p>
  <p>
    {built} functions run today. Each has a reference page with its signature,
    every parameter's type, default, range and units, the fields of its result, an example that ran when the page was
    built, and what it is checked against.
  </p>

  {
    topics.map((t) => (
      <section class="ug-topic">
        <h2 id={t.id}>{t.title}</h2>
        <p>{t.pitch}</p>
        <ul class="ug-fams">
          {t.fams.map((f: any) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`/vipyr/${f.id}`}>
                <code>vp.{f.id}</code>
              </a>{' '}
              {f.about}
            </li>
          ))}
          {t.id === 'data' && (
            <li>
              <code>vp.check_point_in_time</code>, <code>vp.check_parity</code> and <code>vp.compile</code>: the tools
              described under <a href="/vipyr/getting-started#tools">Getting started</a>.
            </li>
          )}
        </ul>
        {t.examples.length > 0 && (
          <div class="ug-cards">
            {t.examples.slice(0, SHOWN).map((e: any) => (
              <GalleryCard entry={e} />
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
        {t.examples.length > SHOWN && (
          <p class="ug-all">
            <a href={`/vipyr/gallery#${t.id}`}>
              All {t.examples.length} in {t.title} →
            </a>
          </p>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <Code lang="sh" code={(REPO_PUBLIC ? `git clone ${GITHUB} && cd viv\n` : '') + 'cargo build --release -p viv\nPYTHONPATH=python python3 -c "import vipyr as vp"'} />
  <p>
    vipyr runs from a checkout of the repository and needs NumPy and a C compiler. The packages are not on PyPI yet;
    <code>pip install vipyr</code> is planned. <a href="/vipyr/install">Install</a> has the details and the
    distributions each domain will ship in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first">A first call</h2>
  <Code lang="python" code={FIRST} />
  <p>
    Arguments are keywords and are checked before anything compiles: a matrix of the wrong shape or a value outside
    its range raises <code>vipyr.ParameterError</code> naming the parameter. The result is a frozen object whose
    fields are documented on the function's page, with units. Your own Viv works the same way:
  </p>
  <Code lang="python" code={OWN} />
  <p><a href="/vipyr/getting-started">Getting started →</a></p>

  <h2 id="checked">How results are checked</h2>
  <p>
    Each function's page says what it is checked against, under <em>Checked against</em>: a reference code (PySCF,
    BIGSTICK, SciPy, mpmath), a published value, or an identity the answer has to satisfy, with the tolerance and the
    reason for it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>check()</code> on a result runs the program's own tests on that run.
    </li>
    <li>
      The default build is designed to give the same bits on every machine: fused multiply-add is off and the math
      functions are Viv's own rather than the platform's. <code>fast=True</code> trades that for speed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every example on these pages ran when the pages were built, and the output shown is what it printed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="viv">The Viv language</h2>
  <p>
    The programs are written in Viv, a small numerical language that compiles to C99 and to WebAssembly. You can use
    vipyr without reading any of it, and step through any of it when you want to see how a result was computed.
    <a href="/tour">The Viv language tour →</a>
  </p>
</DocPage>

<style>
  .ug-logo {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
  }
  .ug-logo img {
    width: 250px;
    max-width: 70vw;
    height: auto;
  }
  .ug-topic {
    margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .ug-fams {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .ug-fams a {
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .ug-fams code {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .ug-cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 1rem 0 0.4rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .ug-cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .ug-cards :global(.g-card h4) {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin: 10px 12px 12px;
  }
  .ug-all {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
